<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Shanghai French Concession</h1><p class="st-meta">1849 CE – 1943 CE · capital Shanghai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The French-governed district of Shanghai, known for its leafy avenues, its gangsters and as the birthplace of the Chinese Communist Party.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> was opened to foreign trade in 1843, the French consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-montigny">Charles de Montigny</a> obtained from the local Chinese authorities in 1849 a concession between the walled Chinese city and the British settlement. When the British and American settlements merged in 1863, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> chose to keep its area separate.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The concession was run by the French consul-general with an elected municipal council, under French law and with its own police. Its tree-lined streets, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huaihai-road">Avenue Joffre</a>, became a favoured residential district. The French police tolerated and employed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-gang">Green Gang</a> bosses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-jinrong">Huang Jinrong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/du-yuesheng">Du Yuesheng</a>, and political exiles found refuge there: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> held its first congress in the concession in 1921.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in 1941 but left the Vichy-run concession in place. In 1943 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vichy-france">Vichy France</a> handed it to the Japanese-backed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wang-jingwei-regime">Wang Jingwei regime</a>, and after 1945 it remained part of Chinese Shanghai.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/shanghai-french-concession.jpg" alt="Shanghai French Concession" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1849 CE – 1943 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shanghai</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Charles de Montigny</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese folk religion, Buddhism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shanghainese, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>French Concession of Shanghai</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1896&amp;focus=shanghai-french-concession" aria-label="Shanghai French Concession on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7818 1040 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Shanghai French Concession lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8138 1296L5054 728M8138 1296L8221 1549"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M8221 1549h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8138 1296h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8138 1296h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1896&amp;focus=shanghai-french-concession">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
